  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThis bilevel optimization strategy addresses the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by first solving it at a lower resolution (n=360) and then upscaling to a higher resolution (n=720). The core insight is that a well-supported solution at a lower resolution serves as a good starting point for higher-resolution optimization, outperforming random initializations. This method balances exploratory searches with targeted refinement, leveraging gradient-based optimization (SLSQP) for low-resolution phases and derivative-free optimization (differential evolution) for high-resolution phases. By separately optimizing each resolution level, we mitigate the curse of dimensionality common with high-resolution methods. Key limitations are the increased computational cost at higher resolutions and reliance on the initial solution quality, but promising results achieve a record low C\u22480.38.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nThe objective minimizes $C_5 = \\frac{2}{n}\\max_{k} \\left| \\int h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \\right|$ over $x \\in [0,2]$ with $n$ evaluation points. We discretize by representing $h$ as a vector $\\mathbf{h} \\in [0,1]^n$ with $\\sum h_i = n/2$. The correlation-based objective is approximated via $\\text{np.correlate}(\\mathbf{h}, 1-\\mathbf{h}, \\text{mode=\"full\"})$, and $C$ is derived by $\\max(\\text{correlation})$ scaled by $2/n$. The optimization is constrained by $0 \\leq h_i \\leq 1$ for all $i$ and $\\sum h_i = n/2$. The bilevel structure decouples the problem into separate optimizations: a sequence of low-resolution (n=360) optimization steps followed by a single high-resolution (n=720) evaluation with repair mechanisms.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n   a. Initialization\n  * Validate n_points \u2208 {360,720} through standardization\n  * If \"initial_h_values\" provided, upscale from its length using NP linear interpolation into target length\n  * Use random initialization otherwise via `random_feasible_vector`\n\n   b. Low-Resolution Optimization (n=360)\n  * Initialize $\\mathbf{h}$ with parent vector\n  * Define objective function: compute_c5(h) = clip(h) \u21a6 np.max(np.correlate(clip(h), 1-clip(h), mode=\"full\")) * (2/len(h))\n  * Set constraint equation: sum_eq(h) = np.sum(h) - n/2\n  * Use SLSQP optimizer for up to 300 evaluations:\n      * Fixed time budget: 300 seconds\n      * Track temperature schedule (start=10, end=0.1, steps=300)\n      * Gradient estimation via finite differences\n      * Repair solution if constraint violated (allow sum deviation \u00b11%)\n\n   c. Upscaling to High-Resolution (n=720)\n  * Interpolate low-resolution solution via:\n      `h_high = np.interp(np.linspace(0,2,720), np.linspace(0,2,360), h_low)`\n  * Enforce sum constraint using $\\text{enforce_sum_bounds}$\n  * Set up objective function and repair mechanisms\n\n   d. High-Resolution Optimization (n=720)\n  * Time budget: 300 seconds\n  * Use differential evolution:\n      * Initialize with interpolated solution\n      * Evaluation budget: 500 iterations\n      * Bounds: [0,1] for all variables\n      * Constraint handling: repair during evaluations only\n      * Objective computation: compute_c5(h)\n\n   e. Validation & Repair\n  * Solution cleaning: clip(h) to [0,1]\n  * Sum repair: enforce_sum_bounds(h, target_sum, max_iter=1000)\n  * Gradient clipping for SLSQP phases: set gradient bounds to 1000\n  * NaN guarding during correlation\n\n   f. Termination\n  * Compare candidates from both resolution phases\n  * Return best valid solution meeting $C<0.38$ threshold\n\n4. Search Choices and Variations\n   a. Temperature Scheduling (low-res phase)\n  * Early-exploration window: $\\tau_0 \\in \\{10,20\\}$\n  * Cooling factor per step: $\\alpha = 0.95$\n  * Deviation threshold: $\\epsilon = 0.01$\n\n   b. Repair Strategies\n  * Low-res: Sum-based adjustment algorithm\n  * High-res: Bounds + sum repair by gradient-free transfer\n  * Fallback to: Most stable candidate from previous cycle\n\n   c. Three Main Variations:\n    i. Balanced (default): Equal time budget split\n    ii. Greedy: Favorentially interpolate only top 10% of parents\n    iii. Randomized: Random interpolation complemented by repair\n\n   d. Adaptive Resolution Feature\n  * Automatic resolution selection from 360-720 based on convergence\n  * Final solution length: $\\min(\\text{original length}, 720)$\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n   a. Dominant Cost Factors:\n    - Correlation calculation: $O(n \\log n)$ per evaluation\n    - Low-res (360): \u2248 10^6 FLOPs\n    - High-res (720): \u2248 2\u00d710^6 FLOPs\n    - Repair overhead: ~2\u00d7 per evaluation\n\n   b. Feasible Schedule:\n    - Phase 1 (low-res): 300s\n    - Phase 2 (high-res): 300s\n    - Early termination: If best C found \u2264 0.385\n\n   c. Robustness Measures:\n    - NaN detection in correlation\n    - Bound enforcement routine\n    - Time-based termination with 5s checkpoints\n    - Stable repair algorithm ensuring $\\mathbf{h}$ remains valid\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\n   a. Feasibility Checks:\n    1. $0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1$ for all $i$\n    2. $|\\text{sum}(h) - n/2| < \\epsilon_{\\text{tol}} = 0.1$\n    3. Return $C < \\infty$\n\n   b. Objective Re-execution:\n    - Indicator value embedded in low_res_obj\n    - Sum of tops checks via repair function\n    - Final solution reevaluated via compute_c5\n\n   c. Fallback Construction:\n    - Returns best available solution if:\n        \u2022 Optimization exceeds time budget\n        \u2022 No valid C_5 found during repair\n        \u2022 Repair exceeds iteration limit\n\n   d. Solution Output Format:\n    [list(float), float, int] of:\n      1. FLattened solution vector\n      2. Computed $C_5$ value\n      3. Solution length (360 or 720)\n\n   e. Family of Related Solutions:\n    - Temperature-parameterized SLSQP variants\n    - Repair-focused interpolation schemes\n    - Gradient-free high-res explorations",
